The schema contains ten distinct entity groupings: an event/action, two actor-role subjects, a named event location, a coordinate point, four levels of enclosing geographic location (admin2, admin1, country, and region, plus optional admin3), an ISO alternate identifier, and a fatality effect. Actor 1 and Actor 2 use separate source columns but share the same actor URI pattern, so the same named actor merges across roles and records.

The event is identified by event_id_cnty and corresponds to bsm:Action. Its stable ACLED identifier maps to bsm:sourceIdentifier; event_date maps to bsm:eventStartDateTime; notes maps to bsm:actionDescriptionText; and disorder_type, event_type, sub_event_type, and interaction are classification values. Here bsm:classificationCode is preferred over the near-synonym bsm:typeName because the subject is an Action and the fields classify an event, whereas typeName is declared for Object. The event links to both actors through bsm:involvedActor, to the event location through bsm:hasGeographicLocation, and to the fatality effect through bsm:hasResult.

Each main actor corresponds conservatively to bsm:Actor rather than bsm:Organisation because values include organizational forces, generic armed groups, and potentially civilians. actor1 and actor2 map to the inherited bsm:conceptName, while inter1 and inter2 map to bsm:typeName. Associated-actor fields are not mapped because they can contain several semicolon-separated actors and the permitted transformations cannot split them safely.

The event location is a bsm:Location identified by its coordinates, with location mapped to bsm:locationName. It links to a bsm:AbsolutePoint carrying latitude and longitude. Geographic containment is represented with bsm:partOf from the event location through optional admin3/admin2, then admin1, country, and region. The ISO field is represented as a bsm:AlternateIdentifier because the field name and description explicitly identify the ISO 3166 standard; this is stronger evidence than a merely generic code column.

Fatalities populate a separate bsm:ActionEffect linked through bsm:hasResult, with a constant fatality description code and fatalities as bsm:effectQuantityNumber. Population fields are not mapped because the ontology has no population property. Precision codes, upload timestamp, source provenance, source scale, civilian-targeting indicator, and tags also lack sufficiently specific matches. In particular, timestamp is an upload time rather than the event or observation time, and source contains publisher names rather than the event’s source identifier.

All IRI transformations were checked against their referenced subjects: actor_{actor1}, actor_{actor2}, location_{latitude}_{longitude}, effect_{event_id_cnty}_fatalities, point_{latitude}_{longitude}, admin3_{country}_{admin1}_{admin2}_{admin3}, admin2_{country}_{admin1}_{admin2}, admin1_{country}_{admin1}, country_{country}, region_{region}, and iso3166_{iso} are character-for-character identical at each reference. No unused transformation key is emitted.

